Another type of newsreader that makes it difficult to read messages with
the crux in the subject line is the type that works in two phases: first
you get a screen with all subject lines, from which you can select the
ones you want to read, then the individual messages are displayed.

Newsreaders like this were among the first available, and since they were
developed for 25x80 ASCII displays, it was common practice to put as many
content lines on a screen as possible. Space could be preserved by not
displaying a subject line, a sender, etc.

This is probably why it became custom to put everything in the message
body: you couldn't make your point if you didn't since lots of people
wouldn't have a clue what you were talking about.

A last consideration could be that it's aesthetically not nice when what
you want to say is split in two parts, usually looking differently as well
(layout-wise).

